-- title:
--   R-points as pairs: an S-point and an Igusa root
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $q$, a prime $\ell$ with $3 \le \ell$, a nonzero natural number $M'$, and a commutative ring $A₀$.
--
--   **Variable-change equivariance over $A₀$.** The hypotheses `hℓ` and `hM` require, for every $A₀$-algebra $T$, every Weierstrass curve $W$ over $T$ and every variable change $C$: that [`ModularCurve.IsLevelPStructure`](def/ModularCurve_KatzLevelP.html#L104) for $\ell$ is preserved when $W$ is replaced by $C \bullet W$ and the four coordinates $(x_P,y_P,x_Q,y_Q)$ of a `LevelPData` are transformed by `LevelPData.variableChange C` (the structure asks that both points satisfy the affine equation, that $\mathrm{pre}\Psi_\ell$ vanishes at both $x$-coordinates, and that the two independence elements `indepElt` are units); and that [`ModularCurve.IsGamma0PowAt W p k h`](def/ModularCurve_WeierstrassGamma0Pow.html#L55) is preserved when $h$ is replaced by `kernelVariableChangeDeg C (gamma0PowDeg p k) h` (this predicate is `IsTwoKernel` when $p^k = 2$ and otherwise `IsCyclicGenKernel`, i.e. $\deg h \le \varphi(p^k)/2$, the coefficient of $h$ in degree $\varphi(p^k)/2$ equals $1$, $h \cdot \mathrm{pre}\Psi_{p^{k-1}} \mid \mathrm{pre}\Psi_{p^k}$, and $h$ divides the relevant `smulNumerator` for all $a$ with $2 \le a \le (p^k-1)/2$ prime to $p$).
--
--   **Group laws, transport and the fine moduli package.** $\mathcal G$ is a family `GroupLaws A₀`, assigning to every $A₀$-algebra $T$ and every projective Weierstrass curve $W/T$ with unit discriminant a relative group law on the structural morphism `projModelStrCR W`; `h𝒢` asserts that this family is chord–tangent (for each such $W$ there is an identification of sections over fields with affine points which is additive and Galois-equivariant), and `h𝒢O` that the identity section is the origin in the origin chart (a ring homomorphism from `OriginChartRing W` cutting it out and killing $x/y$ and $z/y$). $\mathcal T$ is a `LevelTransport A₀ 𝒢 q`, i.e. functorial base-change and variable-change operations on raw Drinfeld pairs (a projective curve together with two sections $P,Q$) compatible with the level condition `RawDrinfeldPair.IsLevel 𝒢 q`, which asks that the pair's curve be the given one and that $P,Q$ be a Drinfeld basis of the $q$-torsion for the group law; `h𝒯` is `IsSectionTransport`, the two clauses pinning the transported sections against variable-change and coefficient homomorphisms on the graded models. $P₀$ is a `LevelModuliPackageAbs` over $A₀$ for the moduli datum attached to `rigidDataPow A₀ ℓ M' q hℓ hM 𝒢 𝒯`, the rigid Weierstrass datum of the product level component $\Gamma_0(M') \times (\Gamma(\ell) \times \text{Drinfeld-}\Gamma(q))$; concretely, $P₀$ consists of an $A₀$-algebra $B₀$ and a point `P₀.univ` over $B₀$ such that for every $A₀$-algebra $T$ every point over $T$ (a variable-change class of a curve with unit $\Delta$, a family of kernel polynomials indexed by the prime factors of $M'$, a level-$\ell$ datum and a raw Drinfeld pair, all satisfying the corresponding level conditions) is the image of `P₀.univ` under a unique $A₀$-algebra map $B₀ \to T$.
--
--   **The ring $R$ and its residue and factorisation data.** $R$ is a Noetherian local $A₀$-algebra, complete for its maximal-ideal-adic topology, and $\iota : B₀ \to R$ is an $A₀$-algebra map. $k$ is a field of characteristic $q$ in which the images of $\ell$ and of $M'$ are nonzero, and $\mathrm{res}_R : R \to k$ is surjective with kernel the maximal ideal of $R$. $W₀$ is a complete discrete valuation domain with maximal ideal generated by $q$, with a surjection $\mathrm{res}_0 : W₀ \to k$ whose kernel is the maximal ideal; $R$ is a $W₀$-algebra, $W₀$ an $A₀$-algebra, these form a scalar tower, and `hresR₀` says $\mathrm{res}_R \circ \mathrm{algebraMap}\, W₀\, R = \mathrm{res}_0$. The clause `hfac` is the universality of $R$: for every Artinian local ring $T$ that is simultaneously a $W₀$- and $A₀$-algebra in the tower, every surjection $\mathrm{res}_T : T \to k$ with kernel the maximal ideal of $T$ and $\mathrm{res}_T \circ \mathrm{algebraMap}\, W₀\, T = \mathrm{res}_0$, and every $A₀$-algebra map $\varphi : B₀ \to T$ with $\mathrm{res}_T \circ \varphi = \mathrm{res}_R \circ \iota$, there is a unique $W₀$-algebra map $\Phi : R \to T$ with $\mathrm{res}_T \circ \Phi = \mathrm{res}_R$ and $\Phi \circ \iota = \varphi$.
--
--   **The étale package over $W₀$.** The hypotheses `hℓ'` and `hM'` are the variable-change equivariance statements of `hℓ`, `hM` with $A₀$ replaced by $W₀$; `h𝒢r` and `h𝒢Or` say that the scalar restriction `𝒢.restrictScalars W₀` is chord–tangent and origin-identity. $P^{\mathrm{et}}$ (`Pet`) is a fine moduli package over $W₀$ for the product level component $\Gamma_0(M') \times (\Gamma(\ell) \times \mathrm{trivial})$ made rigid, $x^{\mathrm{et}}$ (`xet`) is a raw datum over its base ring $P^{\mathrm{et}}.B₀$ (a curve with unit discriminant together with the level data and the level conditions), and `hxet` says that its variable-change class is the universal point of $P^{\mathrm{et}}$.
--
--   **The universal $q$-torsion section.** $C_Q$ is a commutative ring which is a $P^{\mathrm{et}}.B₀$-algebra and a $W₀$-algebra in a scalar tower, and $Q_u$ is a section of the projective model of $x^{\mathrm{et}}.\mathrm{curve}$ over $\operatorname{Spec} C_Q \to \operatorname{Spec} P^{\mathrm{et}}.B₀$. The hypothesis `hQu` says that $q \cdot Q_u$ is the identity section for the restricted group law on that curve, and `hrep` that $(C_Q, Q_u)$ represents $q$-torsion: for every $P^{\mathrm{et}}.B₀$-algebra $T$ and every section $Q$ over $\operatorname{Spec} T$, the relation $q \cdot Q = 1$ holds if and only if there is a unique $P^{\mathrm{et}}.B₀$-algebra map $\chi : C_Q \to T$ such that $\operatorname{Spec}(\chi)$ followed by $Q_u$ is $Q$.
--
--   **The torsion-lift ring $S$ and the Igusa factorisation.** $S$ is a Noetherian local $W₀$-algebra, complete for its maximal-ideal-adic topology, with a $W₀$-algebra map $\iota_S : C_Q \to S$, a surjection $\mathrm{res}_S : S \to k$ whose kernel is the maximal ideal and which satisfies $\mathrm{res}_S \circ \mathrm{algebraMap}\, W₀\, S = \mathrm{res}_0$; `hfacS` is the corresponding universality clause for $S$ over $C_Q$ (Artinian local $W₀$-algebras $T$ with residue map $\mathrm{res}_T$ as above and $W₀$-algebra maps $\varphi : C_Q \to T$ lifting $\mathrm{res}_S \circ \iota_S$ extend uniquely to $W₀$-algebra maps $S \to T$ compatible with the residue maps and with $\iota_S$). $F_S$ is a formal group over $S$ whose power series is the fixed formal group law `formalGroupLawFixed` of $x^{\mathrm{et}}.\mathrm{curve}$ base-changed along $\iota_S \circ \mathrm{algebraMap}\, P^{\mathrm{et}}.B₀\, C_Q$. Further, $g \in S[X]$ is monic of degree $q-1$ with all coefficients in degrees $< q-1$ in the maximal ideal of $S$ and with $g(0) = q \cdot w$ for some unit $w$; $v$ is a unit power series over $S$; and `hfacq` is the factorisation of the $q$-series of $F_S$,
--   $$F_S.\mathrm{nthSeries}\, q \;=\; X \cdot g \cdot v .$$
--   Two further hypotheses are imposed: `hdom`, that $S[X]/(g) =$ `AdjoinRoot g` is an integral domain, and `hqS`, that $q \neq 0$ in $S$.
--
--   **The residual point over $k$.** The field $k$ is made an $A₀$-, $W₀$- and $P^{\mathrm{et}}.B₀$-algebra, with $A₀ \to W₀ \to k$ a tower and `hk₀` identifying the structure map $W₀ \to k$ with $\mathrm{res}_0$. There are an $A₀$-algebra map $\rho : B₀ \to k$ with $\rho = \mathrm{res}_R \circ \iota$ (`hρ`) and a $W₀$-algebra map $\psi^{\mathrm{et}} : P^{\mathrm{et}}.B₀ \to k$ with $\psi^{\mathrm{et}}(b) = \mathrm{res}_S(\iota_S(\mathrm{algebraMap}\, b))$ (`hψet`), and `hψalg` identifies the structure map $P^{\mathrm{et}}.B₀ \to k$ with $\psi^{\mathrm{et}}$. The discriminant of the reduced curve $x^{\mathrm{et}}.\mathrm{curve}$ base-changed along $\psi^{\mathrm{et}}$ is a unit (`hΔk`), and $Q_k$ is a section of that curve over $k$. The pinning hypothesis `hQk_pin` asserts the existence of a graded ring homomorphism $\phi$ from the graded projective model of $x^{\mathrm{et}}.\mathrm{curve}$ to that of its reduction, with the irrelevant ideal of the target contained in the image of the irrelevant ideal of the source, such that $\phi$ is a coefficient homomorphism for $\psi^{\mathrm{et}}$ (`IsCoefficientHom`) and $Q_k$ followed by $\mathrm{Proj}(\phi)$ equals $\operatorname{Spec}(\mathrm{res}_S \circ \iota_S)$ followed by $Q_u$; thus $Q_k$ is the reduction of the universal $q$-torsion section. The hypothesis `hQk` says that $Q_k$ is not the identity section. The level conditions over $k$ are: `hhk`, that for each prime $p \mid M'$ the reduced polynomial $(x^{\mathrm{et}}.\mathrm{level}.1\,p)$ satisfies `IsGamma0PowAt` for $p$ and the exponent $v_p(M')$ at the reduced curve; `hDk`, that the reduced level-$\ell$ datum is a level-$\ell$ structure on the reduced curve; and `hyk`, that the raw Drinfeld pair consisting of the reduced curve, its identity section and $Q_k$ satisfies `RawDrinfeldPair.IsLevel 𝒢 q`. Finally `hρyk` says that the image of `P₀.univ` under $\rho$ in the moduli datum of `rigidDataPow A₀ ℓ M' q hℓ hM 𝒢 𝒯` is the class of the explicit raw datum over $k$ assembled from the reduced curve with unit discriminant, the reduced $\Gamma_0(M')$ and level-$\ell$ data, and the Drinfeld pair (reduced curve, identity section, $Q_k$).
--
--   **Conclusion.** Under these hypotheses there exists a family $\eta$ which assigns to every Artinian local commutative ring $T$ that is a $W₀$-algebra such that the composite $W₀ \to T \to T/\mathfrak m_T$ is surjective, a bijection
--   $$\eta_T : (R \to_{W₀} T) \;\simeq\; \{\, (\Phi, t) \in (S \to_{W₀} T) \times T \;:\; (g^{\Phi})(t) = 0 \,\},$$
--   where $g^{\Phi}$ denotes the image of $g$ under $\Phi$ and the evaluation is at $t$; and this family is natural in the following sense: for all such $T$ and $T'$ (with their surjectivity hypotheses), every $W₀$-algebra map $f : T \to T'$ and every $W₀$-algebra map $\varphi : R \to T$, the pair underlying $\eta_{T'}(f \circ \varphi)$ is $(f \circ \Phi, f(t))$, where $(\Phi, t)$ is the pair underlying $\eta_T(\varphi)$.
--
--   Only the existence of such a natural family is asserted; no formula for $\eta$ is given, and no uniqueness claim is made.
--
--   This identifies the functor of Artinian $W₀$-points of the deformation ring $R$ with the functor of pairs consisting of an $S$-point and a root of the Igusa polynomial $g$ cut out by the factorisation $[q]_{F_S}(X) = X g(X) v(X)$ of the $q$-series of the formal group, naturally in the test ring. It is the step used by [`ModularCurve.LevelModuliPackageAbs.nonempty_algEquiv_adjoinRoot_of_factorsThrough_of_nthSeries_eq_X_mul_mul_of_isDomain_adjoinRoot_gamma0Pow`](thm.html#ModularCurve.LevelModuliPackageAbs.nonempty_algEquiv_adjoinRoot_of_factorsThrough_of_nthSeries_eq_X_mul_mul_of_isDomain_adjoinRoot_gamma0Pow) to obtain an isomorphism of $R$ with $S[X]/(g)$.
